  "query_preview": "..\nStrong winds and dry conditions will bring widespread critical to \nextreme fire weather conditions on Saturday...\n\nToday: Ensembles continue to indicate potential for a high \nimpact fire weather event with strong winds and dry conditions. \nA strong +120kt upper level jet will move across WA as a Low \nswings into the Lower BC. Any chance of precip across the region\nwill limited to the Cascade Crest. The Low will push a strong \ndry cold front through the Inland Northwest with 850mb-500mb \nwinds ranging from 30-60kts. The winds will mix down to the \nsurface as the front passes. Westerly winds will begin to \nincrease in the late morning and continue into the evening. \nTiming for the strongest winds will be around 2 to 8 PDT. \nEnsembles have been very consistent with speeds and timing. For \nspeeds, sustained winds will be in the 20 to low 30 mph range. \nWeaker winds in the teens will be over the Idaho Panhandle. For \ngusts, a range of 30 to low 40s for most. The Idaho Panhandle \nwill have gusts into the 20s. Outside of the Idaho Panhandle, \nprobability of wind gusts greater than 35 mph is at least 60 \npercent. Southeast Washington, Lewiston, and Western Columbia \nBasin including Waterville Plateau down to Wenatchee has 20-40 \npercent probability for greater than 50 mph gusts. Impacts \ninclude blowing dust and minor tree and property damage. Highs \nfor the day will be in the slightly cooler with a range of upper\n70s to low 90s. Overnight lows will be in the 40s to 50s.\n\nSunday: While temperatures will be a lot cooler, winds will \ncontinue to be breezy. The region will see winds sustained in \nthe teens with gusts into the 20s continuing to impact ongoing \nand new fires in the area. Winds will shift to out of the north \nand funnel down the Okanogan across the Waterville Plateau. \nHighs will be in the 70s to mid 80s. Overnight lows will be in \nthe 40s to mid 50s.\n\nMonday through Friday: A flat ridge will fill in behinds the \nexiting Low. It will be in place for most of the upcoming. \nTemperatures will rebound back into the 80s to low 90s. Winds \nwill continue the diurnal trend of breezy afternoons and calm \novernights. The warm, dry pattern will continue with no chances \nof beneficial precip for the start of August. Overnight lows \nwill be in the 50s. /JDC",
